---
// /bot: the page BiasIndexBot's user agent points to, for site owners who find it in their logs.
// The numbers mirror pipeline/fetch_pages.py and .github/workflows/weekly.yml.
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/base-layout.astro"
import { href } from "@/lib/deploy"
import { CONTACT, REPO_URL } from "@/lib/site"
---

<BaseLayout
  title="BiasIndexBot · The Bias Index"
  description="BiasIndexBot reads the comparison pages SaaS companies publish about their rivals, once a week, one request per second."
>
  <main>
    <section class="wrap">
      <div class="doc-title">
        <h1 class="display h1">BiasIndexBot</h1>
        <p>
          If you found <code>BiasIndexBot</code> in your logs: it reads the comparison pages SaaS companies publish
          about their rivals ("X vs Y", "X alternatives"), so <a href={href("/")}>The Bias Index</a> can score how
          one-sided they are.
        </p>
      </div>
      <article class="prose">
        <h2>What it does</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Runs once a week, on Sundays from 05:00 UTC, when most sites are quietest, and identifies itself as
            <code>BiasIndexBot/0.1 (+https://thebiasindex.com/bot)</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Reads your <code>robots.txt</code> and the sitemaps it lists, then fetches only pages whose address looks
            like a comparison (<code>-vs-</code>, <code>alternative</code>, <code>compare</code>…).
          </li>
          <li>
            Makes one request at a time to your site, at least a second apart, or slower if your
            <code>robots.txt</code> sets a <code>Crawl-delay</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Gives each page at most 30 seconds, and stops for the week after three blocked (403, 429, 503) or timed-out pages in a row, rather than retrying harder.
          </li>
          <li>Doesn't run JavaScript, submit forms or log in, and never republishes your pages: it links to them.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Opting out</h2>
        <p>
          <code>BiasIndexBot</code> honors <code>robots.txt</code>. To keep it out entirely:
        </p>
        <pre><code>User-agent: BiasIndexBot
Disallow: /</code></pre>
        <p>
          Your company then shows as "crawler blocked" on the index, with no score: we don't guess what we couldn't read.
        </p>
        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Questions, or a page you think we scored wrong: <code>{CONTACT}</code>. The crawler's code
          is <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/pipeline/fetch_pages.py`}>open on GitHub</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
